  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 318 AM EST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nGOES water vapor imagery and RAP mid-level analysis this morning \nshow a longwave trough traversing CONUS, with low pressure covering \nmuch of the American West and PNW, and high pressure camped out west \nof the Sargasso Sea. Cyclonic southwesterly flow from the Four \nCorners area is helping send Pacific moisture, along with continued \nWAA, toward the Upper Midwest. Nighttime microphysics shows high \nclouds moving off over Lake Superior, followed by mid-level clouds \napproaching the CWA from the southwest. Temperatures are in the low \nto mid 50s central and east, and high 50s to low 60s in the west and \nKeweenaw, which is on track for minimum temperatures 25-30F above \nnormal for tonight. SPC mesoanalysis indicates 925 mb southwesterly \nflow to 50 knots, some of which is mixing down; widespread gusts in \nthe 20-30mph range is the norm tonight. \n\nAs the surface low currently situated over southern Ontario tracks \nnortheast with steering flow over the next day or so, precipitation \ntrails behind it, eventually joining with precipitation associated \nwith the longwave mid-level trough on Sunday. Flow remains \nsouthwesterly, and temperatures remain above average into Sunday. \nGiven well-above freezing temperature profiles and PWAT values \npushing 1\", the west-central UP should receive a soaking rainfall \nlate Saturday through late Sunday. The current NBM run shows an \nupward jump in 24-hour QPF totals, continuing a trend of QPF \nincreases over the past several days. While the highest \ndeterministic totals land around 0.7\"-0.9\", a 60-80% chance \nexists across Marquette, Iron, Dickinson, and Baraga counties of\nreceiving greater than 1\".\n\nTemperatures drop from highs 10-15F above normal (40s & low 50s) \nthroughout Sunday to 5-10F above normal Sunday night (20s & low 30s) \nas a cold front sweeps through. The most abrupt decline in \ntemperatures will likely be felt overnight on Sunday as \nsouthwesterly flow switches to northwesterly, and 850mb temps \ntransition from a prefrontal 7-9C to a postfrontal -4 to -6C \n(dropping further as CAA continues into Monday). Rain will \ntransition to lake effect snow, though significant accumulations are \nnot expected at this time due to antecedent warm ground \ntemperatures, low SLRs, and the quick-hitting nature of favorable \nconditions. With the lake temperature averaging near 8C and initial \nmarginal 850mb temps, snow ratios are likely to start off low Sunday \nnight, near 6-8:1 in the west while winds are still mostly west-\nnorthwesterly. As the wave rotates through and winds shift more \nnortherly, the manifested lake effect band will likewise shift, \nlimiting totals; meanwhile SLRs increase as 850mb temperatures drop \ntoward -11 to -14C throughout Monday. In all, 1-2\" are anticipated \nin the west and northwest lake effect bands on Monday, with \nless near the shorelines. \n\nConditions dry out quickly into Tuesday, however, as high pressure \npushes in early Tuesday. Given anticyclonic flow, temperatures \nremain near seasonable through the mid-week, with highs in the 30s \nand lows in the teens and 20s. At this time, no significant \nprecipitation chances enter the forecast through Thanksgiving \nDay.",
